"CyberSOCEval: Benchmarking LLMs Capabilities for Malware Analysis and Threat Intelligence Reasoning | Research - AI at Meta (https://ai.meta.com/research/publications/cybersoceval-benchmarking-llms-capabilities-for-malware-analysis-and-threat-intelligence-reasoning/)\nciteturn3193view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://ai.meta.com/research/publications/cybersoceval-benchmarking-llms-capabilities-for-malware-analysis-and-threat-intelligence-reasoning/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 242\nL0: #####\nL1: \nL2: ## Products\nL3: \nL4:   * cite0†Muse L5:   * cite1†Meta AI L6:   * cite2†Media Generation L7:   * cite3†Vibes L8: \nL9: #####\nL10: \nL11: ## AI Research\nL12: \nL13:   * cite4†Overview L14:   * cite5†Projects L15:   * cite6†Resources & Tools L16:   * cite7†Publications L17:   * cite8†GitHub†github.com L18: \nL19: #####\nL20: \nL21: ## Resources\nL22: \nL23:   * cite9†Blog L24:   * cite10†Learning Hub L25:   * cite11†Demos†aidemos.meta.com L26: \nL27: #####\nL28: ## About\nL29: \nL30:   * cite12†Overview L31:   * cite13†Open Source L32:   * cite14†Careers†www.metacareers.com L33: \nL34: * cite15†Products L35: * cite15†AI Research L36: * cite15†Resources L37: * cite15†About L38: * cite16†AI Developers†developer.meta.com L39: * cite17†Try Muse†applink.muse.ai L40: * #### RESEARCH\nL41: \nL42: #### DEVELOPER TOOLS\nL43: \nL44: # CyberSOCEval: Benchmarking LLMs Capabilities for Malware Analysis and Threat Intelligence Reasoning\nL45: \nL46: September 15, 2025\nL47: ## Abstract\nL48: Today’s cyber defenders are overwhelmed by a deluge of security alerts, threat intelligence signals, and shifting business context, creating an urgent need for AI systems that can enhance operational security work. Despite the potential of Large Language Models (LLMs) to automate and scale Security Operations Center (SOC) operations, existing evaluations are incomplete in assessing the scenarios that matter most to real-world cyber defenders.\nL49: This lack of informed evaluation has significant implications for both AI developers and those seeking to apply LLMs to SOC automation. Without a clear understanding of how LLMs perform in real-world security scenarios, AI system developers lack a north star to guide their development efforts, and users are left without a reliable way to select the most effective models.\nL50: Furthermore, malicious actors have begun using AI to scale cyber attacks, emphasizing the need for open source benchmarks to drive adoption and community-driven improvement among defenders and AI model developers. To address this gap, we introduce CyberSOCEval, a new suite of open source benchmarks that are part of CyberSecEval 4.\nL51: CyberSOCEval consists of benchmarks tailored to evaluate LLMs in two tasks: Malware Analysis and Threat Intelligence Reasoning, core defensive domains that have inadequate coverage in current security benchmarks. Our evaluations reveal that larger, more modern LLMs tend to perform better, confirming the training scaling laws paradigm.\nL52: We also find that reasoning models leveraging test time scaling do not achieve the boost they do in areas like coding and math, suggesting that these models have not been trained to reason about cybersecurity analysis, and pointing to a key opportunity for improvement. Finally, we find that current LLMs are far from saturating our evaluations, demonstrating that CyberSOCEval presents a significant hill to climb for AI developers to improve AI cyber defense capabilities.\nL53: cite18†Download the Paper†scontent-iad6-1.xx.fbcdn.net L54: #### AUTHORS\nL55: \nL56: Written by\nL57: \nL58: Lauren Deason\nL59: \nL60: Adam Bali\nL61: \nL62: Ciprian Bejean\nL63: \nL64: Diana Bolocan\nL65: \nL66: James Crnkovich\nL67: \nL68: Ioana Croitoru\nL69: \nL70: Krishna Durai\nL71: \nL72: Chase Midler\nL73: \nL74: Calin Miron\nL75: \nL76: David Molnar\nL77: \nL78: Brad Moon\nL79: \nL80: Bruno Ostarcevic\nL81: \nL82: Alberto Peltea\nL83: \nL84: Matt Rosenberg\nL85: \nL86: Catalin Sandu\nL87: \nL88: Arthur Saputkin\nL89: \nL90: Sagar Shah\nL91: \nL92: Daniel Stan\nL93: \nL94: Ernest Szocs\nL95: \nL96: Shengye Wan\nL97: \nL98: Spencer Whitman\nL99: \nL100: Sven Krasser\nL101: \nL102: Joshua Saxe\nL103: \nL104: Publisher\nL105: \nL106: arXiv\nL107: \nL108: Research Topics\nL109: \nL110: cite19†Systems Research L111: \nL112: ### Related Publications\nL113: \nL114: October 02, 2026\nL115: \nL116: #### RESEARCH\nL117: #### Tightness of the Cycle-Based Relaxation for Completed Length-Three Alpha-Cycles\nL118: \nL119: Aykut Arslan\nL120: \nL121: October 02, 2026\nL122: \nL123: cite20†Read the Paper L124: \nL125: October 02, 2026\nL126: \nL127: #### RESEARCH\nL128: \nL129: #### On Solvable Evolution Algebras and a Conjecture by García-Martínez and Pérez-Rodríguez\nL130: \nL131: Andres Barei Bueno\nL132: \nL133: October 02, 2026\nL134: \nL135: cite21†Read the Paper L136: \nL137: October 02, 2026\nL138: \nL139: #### RESEARCH\nL140: #### String Two-Point Function = Height Function on a Curve\nL141: \nL142: Anindya Dey, Gabriel Herczeg, An Huang, Nicolas Jaramillo Torres, Jacob H. Swenberg\nL143: \nL144: October 02, 2026\nL145: \nL146: cite22†Read the Paper L147: \nL148: October 02, 2026\nL149: \nL150: #### RESEARCH\nL151: \nL152: #### Semiabelian Groups Need Not Be Monomial\nL153: \nL154: Joseph Phillip Brennan, Milana Golich\nL155: \nL156: October 02, 2026\nL157: \nL158: cite23†Read the Paper L159: \nL160: cite24†See All Papers L161: \nL162: cite25†Image†scontent-iad3-2.xx.fbcdn.net L163: \nL164: ## Help Us Pioneer The Future of AI\nL165: ##### We share our open source frameworks, tools, libraries, and models for everything from research exploration to large-scale production deployment.\nL166: \nL167: cite26†Join our Team L168: \nL169: cite1†Meta AI L170: \nL171: cite1†Assistant L172: \nL173: cite